    "q-344034": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344034",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $3$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{5}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
